The smoke in my mouth tastes like giving up.
It colors the sky a sickly yellow. On higher ground, where the flat Oregon countryside sprawls, the sun can still be seen. But here, down near the Columbia River, with the highway plunging through the gorge, the smoke has settled like a layer of sediment.
I can’t help but muse on extraterrestrial archaeologists. If they found us petrified here, our suffering rendered timeless in soot, would they see the justice in it? Wildfires demand accountability. I don’t know how they started, but I don’t mind being the one held responsible.
The melodrama of voluntary martyrdom is a bit much, I guess, but the guilt comes easy nonetheless. Especially when leaving behind friends and family with three states burning. Shade and I didn’t plan it this way, the fires just came to life at the worst possible time, in the handful of days leading up to our departure. The rite of return can only be performed on the night before a new moon, and only at the site of the burial. A thousand miles from home. So we watched the emergency updates aggregate as time and indifference drew in our deadline, along with the expanding evacuation zone. Yellow, like the smothering sky.
We could not quiet the fear of returning to find everything in ash, so the car rides heavy. We’re hauling along not only the necessary conduits for the rite of return, but all the belongings we could stuff into suitcases and duffle bags and old Amazon boxes. We packed in stages, each one deepening like the circuits of hell. The irreplaceable things, the valuable things, the convenient things. We filled the leftover spaces with whatever would fit best. Leaving everything else behind. The things I couldn’t protect.
That absolutely feels like running away, and if I’m already admitting my failures, I might as well take responsibility for the wildfires on top of it. I can’t find anyone else willing to answer for all of this, at least not out here on the highway.
More than that, I just cannot fucking see. I’ve maintained a cautious relationship with the accelerator and a generous one with the brake. I’ve kept the music low, too, but that has less to do with other drivers. I can’t see the river, but I’d at least like to hear if we drive past a golem.
I grew up near golems, like most anyone from the country, and they populate so many of my memories of that place. Plains golems can generally displace a large pickup truck, but a simple set of rites keeps them from trampling livestock and produce, and their timid disposition keeps them from wandering across roadways or into populated areas. But they fill all the horizons in between, marking baseball games with their passage in the distance, lining the highways I once rode to visit my childhood sweetheart, decorating water towers as provincial mascots. The ubiquity of their bovine bulk sort of flattens their enormity. Growing up I never considered that it might feel different to witness a golem of any other order.
But a river golem evokes something far wilder. They don’t strike an immediately imposing figure, with most of their eighty-foot height concealed beneath the water, and their territorial dispositions make for infrequent sightings, but the ease with which they navigate the river speaks to an unfathomable physicality. To displace all that churning mass so quickly requires such a tremendous outpouring of violence, it's impossible to imagine anything in the natural world bringing to them any kind of harm. Except for time, I suppose, and the river itself.
“Where are we?” my passenger asks.
I turn reflexively.
Shade lies with their seat reclined, arms clasped over a notebook. I recognize the design on the cover. I picked it up earlier this year, on an evening when Shade’s depression had crested over a painful peak. As a gift it isn’t much to speak of, but it was the best I could do on my way home from work. I hadn’t noticed that Shade grabbed it while packing, but I’m glad they did.
“We’re still in Oregon,” I reply. “You’ve only been asleep for like an hour.”
Shade clicks their seat into a more upright position. They rub their eyes, then look out the window. I rub my neck and add to my comment.
“I say an hour like it’s no time at all, but honestly, after that much driving, I kind of figured we’d be able to see the sky by now.”
Shade shakes their head.
“No. We’ll have to go a lot further than this.”
“Yeah?”
“The particulate matter that comprises wildfire smoke can vary pretty widely. It depends on what’s getting burned up, of course. We’ve got a lot of coniferous trees here in Oregon, which means a lot of resin. That’s why the smoke is so thick.”
“What you’re saying is, Oregon timber could rival the sappy sound of a Stradivarius, if only it wasn’t going up in smoke.”
Shade blinks.
“That was horrible.”
“Yeah,” I admit.
Shade continues, undaunted.
“There’s a similar sort of forest in northern Russia that caught fire a few months ago. You’d assume it would burn differently, that slow growth in a colder climate would make for denser wood, but density is defined at a cellular level. The environment has an impact, but it’s negligible.”
“Nature over nurture, then? That’s awfully fatalistic.”
Shade shakes their head.
“The science doesn’t say things can’t change. Stradivarius worked with ordinary wood, not the frames of cathedrals or materials made resonant by an ice age, but perfectly ordinary wood, and transformed it into something beautiful and evocative.”
“So I was wrong. What’s the science actually say?”
“Just that, no matter what happens, inherent truths cannot be changed.”
I think about this for as long as I can while the road rolls along beneath the car. The smoke thins, just a little. The highway turns away from the river, leading us into the countryside. I encounter more motorists and focus on contending with them. I know I’m a safe driver, I’m proud of my ability to take us anywhere without worry. We pass beyond Boise and I forget all about whatever had held my focus so rapt.
We stop for gas before leaving Idaho. I come around to the passenger’s side of the car and step into a practiced moment of frustration. My shoulders slump. I sigh. Not a ritual, but definitely a routine.
I stare at the dent in the car for only a fraction of a second. It doesn’t stare back. I don’t need to put up any defenses, it isn’t a phantom, just an artifact. I clipped a cement pylon in a parking garage just about a year ago. I had owned this car for only a couple months back then, had lectured myself at length about taking better care of it than its predecessor. The percussive keening of metal on cement had sounded like so much familiar antipathy from the ex I couldn’t escape.
The digital tally on the pump ticks upward. Shade pops open their door to ask if I can grab a snack from inside the station’s convenience store. They probably mean gummy bears, but I second guess myself before they withdraw.
“Sweet snack?”
Shade nods.
“Uh huh. Gummy bears.”
The convenience store is larger on the inside than I expected. I steer myself through the aisles and grab what I need. As I loop back around toward the cash register I pass by a display of luminescent tchotchkes. Small shapes dangle with gemstone translucence and insect wing venation. They glow only faintly, emanating a warm light discordant against the overhead fluorescent bulbs. An advertisement proclaims them fae glamours, touting the subtle but striking ways they might modify my appearance.
I grab one off the display. It feels so light, almost like it could float from my grasp. I walk over to the beer fridge, hoping that the glass will offer a workable reflection, then remember that no one can see their own glamours.
But even if I could see them, what changes would I care about? I don’t know. I can’t think of anything I find lacking in my appearance, at least, not anything that I couldn’t improve upon with a slightly more rigorous self-care routine. My reflection is faint, but I like what I see. Besides, real power doesn't come from making the audience feel differently about the player, it comes from changing how they feel about themselves.
There’s a day I can recall with such clarity that the feelings come wholly undiminished, prepared like a well-tailored outfit made pliable through use.
Earlier that week I had told my ex, then partner, that I felt a little anxious about the schedule for the upcoming weekend. She shrugged off my concern with a casual certainty I couldn’t refute. After all, my exploratory therapy appointment would end in the early morning, leaving plenty of time for an oil change and brake check before I needed to drive my ex to her own therapy appointment. I followed through with my original plan.
The therapy went well enough, but ran a little late. I messaged my ex, just to keep her aware of my timeframe. The oil change started promptly, but before too long a mechanic pulled me aside in the lobby. I messaged my ex regarding this development too, wanting to keep her updated.
While removing a wheel to inspect the brake beneath, the mechanic had watched the caliper fall out in pieces. No car in that condition could safely return to the road. I would need to wait until a replacement was installed, and the necessary part would take at least an hour to arrive, maybe longer.
My ex spent the next four hours messaging me to call out my cringing failure to confront the mechanic about their obvious grift; my inability to plan effectively when scheduling multiple errands; and my general lack of care for her needs compared to my own.
I arrived home half an hour too late to help my ex get to her appointment on time. I apologized to the best of my ability, taking responsibility for my failures and trying hard to avoid offering any defensive excuses for what had happened.
I had a hard time meeting her eyes as she explained to me that, like so many other traits of mine, my hyperfixation on my own failures actively and continually brought misery to everyone else in my life. The obsessive negativity I carried with me made my company unbearable. It drove away both my friends and hers.
This felt so strange to hear, deeply at odds with the things I understood about myself, but if these sentiments didn't hold any merit, why would my partner be sharing them?
I return the tchotchke to the display.
Shade and I continue through Idaho and stop for the night somewhere in Utah. The town feels awful, oppressed by the miasma that accumulates wherever too many rites of return remain unfinished. The broken pylon signs and sprawling vacant lots speak to other pervading hardships, but the afflictive leeching that sits just outside my natural sensory periphery is unmistakable.
“Why do you think that happens?” I ask.
Shade looks up from their phone.
“Hm? Why does the what happen?”
We’ve just finished unloading the full contents of the car into tonight's hotel room. I probably would have made sure we emptied out the car even in a less hostile environment, but this place especially has earned my distrust. There's a set of rough concrete stairs in the hallway outside our room, which lead up to a bricked over doorway. I can't convince myself that I'll still find it there in the morning.
“The …”
I rub my neck.
“Grossness, in places where the rite of return doesn’t get performed enough.”
Shade and I have put words to so many of our feelings in the short time we’ve known each other, it surprises me that we haven’t made it to this conversation.
“It feels like you have an opinion you want to share,” Shade remarks.
I laugh.
“Don’t I always?”
“Yes. So, go on.”
“Well,” I pause to shrug, “I guess it just feels like it has to be a punishment, y’know? Punishment for the neglect of responsibility. Or something like that.
“It's not like anyone can just go ahead and perform the rite of return for anyone else. There’s no, uh, ordained returner, that’s not how this works. We’ve got to choose the person we want to perform our rite, like, way before we die. We give this specific task deliberately to a single individual. It is solely their own responsibility. And if they won’t do their fucking job, well, they should get punished. Failure leads to punishment. Especially on a larger scale.”
Shade sits for a moment, processing this, then speaks slowly.
“This makes sense. I understand why you’d feel this way.”
“Feel this way?” I ask.
Shade nods before explaining.
“You spent a decade as the sole caretaker for your chronically ill abuser, without any support system of your own. Of course that experience would color the way you feel about lots of things.”
“Well that’s …”
I struggle to find a better way to finish the sentence.
“That’s a lot.”
“Yeah, it is,” Shade agrees.
I let the sentiment hang in the air for as long as I can handle it.
“So what's your opinion?” I ask at last.
Shade’s expression takes a shape I can’t quite read.
“I think it has more to do with sadness than punishment.”
“Sadness?”
“Mmhmm. The communal cultivation of sadness, until it puts down roots and becomes part of the landscape.”
“Communal? Like, individuals failing their community? That doesn’t seem different, just worse.”
“Maybe. I dunno. I just think it’s sad when people feel like they can’t ask for help and then harm themselves in the aftermath.”
The following morning comes slowly, the sunrise muted by the haze still clinging to the sky. The window in our room faces west, and through the drawn curtains I watch the world gradually brighten. I imagine that I can see back down the way we’ve come.
A fable comes to mind. The King and the Crevasse. I’ve never liked it.
The ruler of a grand kingdom wakes up every morning, hauls his bleary-eyed ass out to the edge of a cliff, and stands with his toes hanging out into empty space. Ostensibly he’s doing this as a test, to make sure he still has all the self-possession needed to rule a kingdom. But the only thing I can think is how he must find that view miserable and exhausting.
A few hours later we’re somewhere in Wyoming. The land stretches unbroken in all directions, vast and formidable and still somehow dwarfed by the vault of the sky. I don’t feel exposed, though. I feel claustrophobic, penned in by all that emptiness. Through the intermittent line of snow fences and the occasional, distant golem, I can see only nothing.
I realize that I forgot to check for that strange doorway in the hotel this morning.
“You were right,” I say, breaking an extended period of silence.
“What?”
I rub my neck before I continue.
“You were right, I think. About the rite of return, and the reason why things get weird when it goes unfinished too many times.”
I’ve caught their interest, and Shade sits up.
“Why do you say that?”
“I’ve been thinking about it, and like, things also get weird around industrial farms too, right?”
“Right. This is a proven fact.”
“So obviously, in some way, animals have to perform their own rites of return, however that works out. If they didn’t have their own rites, then in places where animals die in confinement, where they’re used as livestock, things wouldn’t like, get weird there.”
Shade giggles. I raise an eyebrow. They shake their head reassuringly before explaining.
“That phrase you keep using. Things get weird. That’s probably the silliest way I’ve ever heard that phenomenon described.”
I think for a moment.
“It’s pretty strange that we don’t have a word for it, yeah?”
“It is.”
“Huh. Well. Since things get weird around industrial farms, it can’t really be because the animals are punishing each other, right?”
“No?”
“Of course not. They aren’t even given the opportunity to perform, well, probably any rites. They’re not going to punish each other over something they’ve been rendered incapable of doing by circumstances beyond their control.”
Shade thinks on this.
“Maybe they’re punishing the farmers.”
Wyoming eventually gives way. Instead of taking the interstate south into Colorado, I turn off at Laramie and follow a smaller three-digit route. The landscape changes almost immediately. Massive lesions scar the earth, red bones protrude for the elements to punish. I’m amazed that a place in the heart of the land where I live can feel so alien. We pass through the shadow of a cantilevered rock shelf as big as the town where I grew up, and here I might as well hold witness to the remains of a doomed starship.
What would the end have looked like for these starfolk? A rush of sound. A flare of heat. Centuries of silent petrification. With no one around to perform a rite of return. Perhaps they wouldn't need it. They came from a whole different world, after all. I don’t know how magic works out there. Not that I really know how it works here, either. If we left right now, for the moon or Mars or beyond, could we reach a distance where this place no longer held any power over us? If I died somewhere out in the Andromeda galaxy, would that free my friends or family from the burden of performing my rite of return?
It takes tremendous effort to part roots from soil, it takes so much fuel and fire to leave orbit. What a staggering cost to reach the unknown.
Denver passes by as a blur of lights. The road construction further south delays our arrival, and we pull into the cemetery outside Colorado Springs shortly after nine o’clock. The sun set hours ago. The inert sky hangs dark above us, absent both moon and stars. The rampant haze glows, polluted by artificial light.
Shade and I work without speaking, unloading the conduits from the car. There isn’t much to carry, the rite of return requires only a handful of ingredients. Shade carries a Mason jar in one hand and a couple grocery bags in the other, while I swing the flashlight back and forth, illuminating one headstone after the next, until we find the name we’re looking for.
Shade lays out the conduits alongside the soft, tilled earth.
“Andrew Sherman Holbrook,” they read aloud.
“That’s quite the mouthful,” I observe.
“My family used to joke that grandma had named uncle Andrew hoping he’d become a writer of great literature, not an author of children’s comics.”
“That’s a pretty bad joke.”
Shade shrugs.
“There’s a reason I’m the one performing the rite.”
We stand for another second, but there isn’t much left to do except start.
I hold the flashlight for Shade while they arrange the conduits. They begin by unscrewing the lid of the Mason jar and splashing the contents out onto the grave. Clear water glints briefly in the flashlight's hard beam. Shade scatters the spot with a bundle of shorn wheat, followed by a bundle of burnt twigs. Black dust smears their hands, phantom appendages nearly swallowed by the surrounding night.
They step onto the grave and stand with palms open, their head raised toward the sky.
“Water of the welling, blood returned. Wheat of the reaping, flesh returned. Wicker of the kindling, bones returned. I am the mote in every star’s eye. Give unto me the truest name of the one here unlofted, I will return them to you.”
I know what comes next. I’ve never heard it myself, never performed a rite that requires access to the oldest truths, but everyone’s at least read about it. I try to imagine the sensations as Shade experiences them.
The chime of a bell rings out, quiet despite its clarity, as though sounding from a great distance. It swells, not through amplification, but proximity. It bears down, roaring, an uncompromising crescendo too vast and too certain to understand things like trust and pity and forgiveness.
Shade’s open palms clench into fists, and until they let go they’ll know Andrew Sherman Holbrook’s truest name.
They raise their fists, extend both their index fingers, and draw a series of sigils in the air. A ring of light opens above them, just out of reach. In its center I can see the naked sky, unobscured by layers of smoke. Shade holds their position for a moment and then begins to spin. They take measured steps, careful to keep their head up and maintain their balance. The sky within their circle of light follows in turn. The stars trace their circuits backward, etching bright lines with their passage. The conduits sink into the earth. The conjured magic pushes or pulls them downward, deeper and deeper as Shade works the rite. The patterns carved into the grave look to me like some excavated prehistoric canyon.
Then the stars above Shade reach the position they held on the day Andrew was born. 
Shade lets go.
We spend the night in Colorado Springs and sleep the spacious, spartan sleep of true exhaustion. The morning comes just slowly enough for me to steel my resolve before getting us back on the highway. I follow the interstate this time, driving up into Cheyenne before turning west. This takes us through scenery much less engaging than yesterday’s detour, but I only notice the lack in retrospect.
Shade talks at length about their uncle, and I really do my best to pay attention, but my unshakable preoccupation keeps me from participating in any meaningful way. Shade’s a good sport, they allow me to reply with token affirmations as they carry the rest of the conversation themselves. I greatly appreciate it but can’t find the energy to express this.
Anxiety monopolizes my thoughts. The smoke still hasn’t cleared, and I wish like hell that it would. But fire is patient. I haven’t checked the news since we left, have barely even found the strength to look at my phone. I can only guess at how far the wildfires have spread. The plume above might right now carry the ashes of the house that I own.
I haven’t lived there for a while, that isn’t the place I left behind when I started this trip with Shade. My ex lives in the house instead, its sole occupant since the dissolution of our relationship. Well, her and all the pets.
The house sits much closer to the heart of the wildfires than my apartment. An emergency update inducted the address into the red evacuation zone a couple days before Shade and I left town. I was able to move my ex and the pets out to stay with a friend of hers, but that could only ever be a temporary solution. What if I get back and the house is gone?
At some point we stop to eat. I take my time, replenish my spent stamina. Getting off the highway helps reset my looping patterns of thought, and the calories help me focus. For a while I find that I can engage, even after we start back up on the road. Shade and I agree that neither of us wants to spend another night in that strange Utah podunk, so before we leave Wyoming I turn off the interstate and head north toward Pocatello.
Evening comes quickly after this. The diminishing light takes with it the last of the energy I have for interaction. I ask Shade to turn on some music. Dreamy folk-pop carries us up into the higher country of the Wyoming Range. It carries Shade off to sleep, too, but I don’t mind. The road imposes heavy demands on my attention.
The subtleties of the sunset blur together so that night falls all at once. The darkness staggers my senses. It feels like some organic thing has crept into the landscape to blot it out. The dashboard's electronic display dims. The music begins to rasp. My foot presses down on the pedal.
I don’t need to go faster. The roadside guardrail flails back and forth, identifying through separation all the spaces beyond that swallow whole whatever light they receive. I don’t want to go faster.
Headlights glare in the opposing lane. A freightliner tumbles toward me. I barely have time to squeeze the steering wheel before it careens past in a silent rush. The car shakes with the force of it. I glance into the rearview mirror and watch the monster recede into distortion. Another pair of headlights sweeps the night ahead of me. I don’t want to go faster.
This next truck surges past. More headlights. Then more headlights. And again. This rhythmic strobing illuminates nothing, some veil now hides the interior of the car. I don't want to go faster.
All I want to do is stop, but I can’t seem to remember how. Maybe driving is just supposed to be like this? If I can’t figure out how to stop, what else am I supposed to believe?
The final truck passes and everything crackles to life. I can see my hands again, and the cabin around me, and Shade sleeping in the passenger seat. There's something else here, too. The night sits with us, coiled among the belongings in the back of the car. It watches, only moving to breathe.
Several moments pass before I realize why I still can't hear the road. The speakers have started playing music again. Not what Shade picked out, but I recognize the song immediately.
I try to slow down and still can’t manage it.
My ex and I hosted our wedding at a park out in the country. On the morning of, I drove alone into the city to pick up our cupcakes before catching up with everyone else. This album was waiting in the cd player, so I let it play through. My mind wandered over the preceding few days, and the album’s opening track had barely trailed to a close when I realized that the last thing my grandmother would ever do in her life was attend my wedding.
I try to slow down and still can’t manage it, my attention now divided between the road ahead and the churning shape in the back seat.
My father’s mother flew out to Oregon with two of my cousins a couple of nights before the wedding. She had too little energy to spare the first night, so I took some time the following evening to see her. I didn’t understand then, and honestly still don’t now, why she made the trip. I hadn’t made space in my life to visit any of my extended family in more than half a decade. I didn’t deserve this affection.
I try to slow down and the new passenger leans forward, the vaporous shape of it gusting as though blown or pulled by some wind.
My grandmother pulled me in for a welcoming hug and I cried at once, overwhelmed by the size of her, dismayed that a person that large should have to subsist in a body so small, that time and disease should be allowed to do this to any of us. 
I try to slow down and feel the passenger touch the back of the headrest.
All that and it still took me another full day before I realized that my grandmother had chosen to attend my wedding with the final days of her life. I hadn’t given her anything, not my time, not even a word, in years. She sacrificed the very last of herself in order to help celebrate a relationship I couldn’t even manage to maintain.
I try to slow down and the passenger pours into the front seat.
It really isn’t surprising, though. My ex pointed this out, again and again, and I refused to take it to heart. I never give any consideration to anyone else. I do whatever I find most convenient and then play the victim if the consequences happen to catch up. I decided I found it too hard to take care of someone else so I abandoned them. I disavowed all the years that everyone else had shed for me and just ran away.
I try to slow down and the feeling of the passenger pressed against my body triggers a spasm in my throat.
The charitable fraternity comes slowly. The passenger knows me, knows I understand when it speaks, even though it uses a language I’ve half forgotten. It issues grave commands it knows I will fulfill. My body reacts, tensing, in all the habitual ways, and my mind conjures up the anvil on which I will temper myself further.
I’m so tired.
I lean forward and lay my head on the steering wheel. My breath comes out so softly, the last words of a drifting astronaut. My protection has failed and I have no way back. I don’t want to let anyone down, but the last little huff of air fogging my helmet has already started to fade. I just don’t have anything left. My hands fall from the wheel and one of them brushes Shade’s thigh.
The passenger in the back has left and the car idles alongside the road. I can hear the hum of the engine. I raise my head and stare for a long moment at the weedy grass waving beyond the headlights.
The click of the car door doesn’t wake Shade, so I push it wide and step out into the night. The stars remain hidden. The air still smells of smoke. I walk away from the car, keeping the beacon brightness of it behind me, and don’t stop until distance nearly obscures it.
The rite of return, like many other pieces of magic, actually functions only as an aggregate of several smaller rites. Anyone can invoke an individual rite without bringing about any greater effect, especially when a rite doesn’t require a conduit.
I ball my hands into fists and raise them as high as I can reach, then point with my index fingers and draw a familiar series of sigils. A ring of light opens above me. I stall for a moment, seeing the stars. I hold them gently, fearful of leaving bruises. Then I start turning, charting out a stellar tracery, drawing with each step the radial history of the sky. The stars spin backward, back and back, leaving unwavering trails in their wake, the inviolate memory of everything. The lines begin to intersect. I keep turning, scuffing up clouds of dust, a primordial offering to nothing in particular. I lose track of time. The sky keeps spinning, all the way back, until the circular gate has filled with light and I stand in a time before any mistakes were ever made.
The night that we spend in Pocatello passes quietly. Shade either can’t recollect or doesn’t want to mention the events of the drive, and I don’t really have it in me to go through the details anyway. Our mutual languor persists as we start the final push toward home. The thought of finishing this drive in silence disturbs me, but exhaustion wins out. I let the car put the highway to use, my mind settling into dissociation.
It takes a moment or two before I recognize Shade’s voice.
“Are you okay?”
I reckon with this. My thoughts form a strange geometry and translation proves difficult. I want to be honest.
“I don’t know,” I admit.
“I thought so. Do you want to talk about it?”
My focus sharpens by degrees. We’re closing in on Boise. I think. Maybe we’ve already passed it. It doesn’t make much difference. The landscape here evokes a tangible antagonism and my memory draws the connection. Or maybe that's just me. It seems fair, in the midst of conflagration and desertion, that I shouldn’t find a place of solace.
I thought for so long that I could find healing in leaving, that my weary heart could recover if only I could get away. From her. I didn’t just daydream this fiction, I let it smolder as an obsession until it shaped all my waking thoughts. I stumbled in search of an oasis, a man consumed, begging to be led to water.
I’d started with hope, probably, but it had withered long before the frenzied weeks that precipitated my departure. Through nothing more noble than my own chronic apathy and the damage it inflicted, I managed to pull myself free. The white walls and single mattress pad of an unfurnished apartment replaced the visions of celebration I had tirelessly entertained. My deepest yearning came true, and what did I find? Nothing but the unequivocal confirmation of every wretched trait she saw in me.
Even now, tumbling through these thoughts a hundred miles away from her, I can hear her response. The accusation. I need to stop moralizing everyone’s criticism. I’m just reframing the narrative so that I can play the victim. And God, I don’t want her to be right, but I just don’t know anymore. Every choice I make brings so much misery to the people in my life, but all I can do with that knowledge is think about how much pain I’m in. Me. Not them. I break promises and run from responsibility and whine about how sad I am.
At some point we cross back into Oregon. I have no idea how long I’ve made Shade wait for my reply. Typical.
“I’m sorry,” I plead. “I’m not trying to run away. I just--”
“Run away? From what?”
Shade sounds genuinely confused, which surprises me.
“From this conversation, I mean. I promise I’m not trying to avoid you.”
“Is that really what you think you’re doing? Do you really think you’re running away from me?”
This catches me off guard, and I pause to reflect.
Our surroundings have largely flattened. We drive past cell towers and industrial irrigation systems and signs preparing us for intermittent road work. The highway curves and then straightens, bringing us up alongside the Columbia River. A layer of smoke hangs low in the sky. It looks nearly impenetrable, but doesn’t waft down to cover the earth. Shade waits for me to continue.
“I guess I feel like I’m running away so I don’t have to confront the truth about myself.”
Shade interrupts before I can draw breath to continue.
“Pull over.”
“What?”
“Pull over. We can keep talking about this, but I need you to pull over first.”
I don’t understand, but neither do I object. Mostly I just can’t. The road cuts through a pair of steep escarpments, offering no shoulder whatsoever. For a moment it feels like driving back through the Wyoming Range, but I catch myself. A solid wall is not a yawning void.
“I need a minute,” I explain, as though this weren’t obvious.
Shade doesn’t reply until the water comes into view.
“This will probably work.”
“Fuck,” I agree.
A river golem dominates the center of my vision. It stands with improbable and unrivaled authority. Briefly I indulge a fantasy of safety, found within the dominion of its gaze, which must stretch so incredibly far. In the deep distance, near the limits of my own sight but certainly not the golem’s, I can see where the overhead mantle of smoke terminates in a hard, straight line, as though severed from some greater part, forced through action to come to an end. A ribbon of vivid blue hangs above the horizon, and in that space the sun arcs downward. I haven’t seen a clear sunset in over a week.
I pull over at the nearest overlook. My body moves as though compelled, steering me away from the car and toward the river. The air feels cool despite the lingering humidity. A breeze blows in from off the water. The golem towers like a reaching limb.
It stands unchallenged by the river’s insistence, utterly certain of where it belongs. For a moment I consider that I might know less here than I thought, that the golem might be using magic to repel the water, but the waves paint a dark, uneven line of dampness on the golem's surface. It endures.
Ten years I've lived in Oregon. I can count with fingers to spare the number of times I’ve seen one of these.
Finally I realize that Shade has followed me from the car.
“God I’m selfish,” I blurt.
I mean to offer an apology, but Shade cuts me off by drawing me into a hug. I sigh, my breath rattling, and lean in.
“You don’t have to do this,” I say at last.
“What?”
I lift my head to unmuffle my voice.
“You don’t have to do this. My emotional labor, I mean. I’m spent, all used up, but I’m the one who threw away my tinder. This isn’t your responsibility.”
Shade puts both their hands on my shoulders and steps back, giving me a steady stare.
“You just spent a week driving your friend halfway across the country and back, in the middle of a historic wildfire, with basically no rest to speak of. I’m not doing your emotional labor, because I don’t have to. You’re already this person. I just want you to be able to see it.”
I hold their gaze, then look toward the golem. I reach to rub my neck and hear the catch in my breath even before my hand flinches away. I stare at my fingers as the pain begins to register. They ache as though burned.
